---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import LegalIdentity from '../../components/LegalIdentity.astro';
import { route } from '../../i18n/routes';
import { AUTHOR_NAME, AUTHOR_DESC, CONTACT_EMAIL, SITE_NAMES } from '../../data/site-config';
import { srcLink } from '../../lib/helpers';
import { KIT as K } from '../../lib/kit';
import { profilePageSchema, breadcrumbSchema } from '../../lib/seo';
const lang = 'en';
const path = route('about', lang);
const title = 'Radif Partners, publisher of this 2027 holiday calendar';
const description = 'The 2027 and 2026 holiday calendar on this site is prepared by Radif Partners: holidays read in the BOE and each official gazette, no account, no tracking.';
const s = (k: string, t: string) => srcLink(k, lang, t);
---
<PageLayout title={title} description={description} lang={lang} jsonLd={[profilePageSchema({ path, lang }), breadcrumbSchema([{ name: 'Home', path: route('home', lang) }, { name: 'About', path }])]} breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'About' }]}>
  <article class="prose prose-slate max-w-none">
    <h1>About</h1>
    <p class="text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-wide text-navy-500">Independent, non-governmental site</p>
    <p>{AUTHOR_DESC.en}</p>
    <h2>Why {SITE_NAMES[lang]} exists</h2>
    <p>Knowing which days are holidays in your Spanish town takes three texts today: the BOE resolution with its 19-column table, your region's decree and the local-holiday resolution, which in many provinces comes out in a different gazette. The articles that do the rounds each autumn copy the national table and rarely give your town's days. Here you type your town and see its 14 holidays, each with its source next to it, in Spanish or in English.</p>
    <h2>Who publishes it</h2>
    <p>It is published by {AUTHOR_NAME}, a company that builds tools from public rules and data. It is not a public body, a payroll agency or a law firm. The site has no link to the Ministry of Labour, the regional governments or the town councils, and uses none of their crests or logos. It charges for nothing and carries no affiliate links.</p>
    <LegalIdentity lang={lang} />
    <h2>Where the dates come from</h2>
    <p>National and regional holidays: the Directorate-General for Labour's yearly resolutions, <Fragment set:html={s('boe-2027', 'for 2027')} /> and <Fragment set:html={s('boe-2026', 'for 2026')} />. Local holidays: each region's or province's resolution, cited with issue and date on each region's page. The framework: the <Fragment set:html={s('et', 'Workers\' Statute')} /> and <Fragment set:html={s('rd2001', 'Royal Decree 2001/1983')} />. The clock change: <Fragment set:html={s('rd236', 'Royal Decree 236/2002')} />. We cover {K.MUNICIPIOS.length} towns today. How we work is in the <a href={route('method', lang)}>method</a>; how we correct, in the <a href={route('editorial', lang)}>editorial policy</a>.</p>
    <h2>Your data</h2>
    <p>The tools run only in your browser: the town you search for, the leave days you enter and the .ics file you download never reach a server. More in the <a href={route('privacy', lang)}>privacy policy</a>.</p>
    <h2>What the site does not do</h2>
    <p>It does not replace the calendar your employer must display at the workplace or your collective agreement, which may set personal days, shifts or closures. It gives no employment advice.</p>
    <h2>Write to us</h2>
    <p><a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>, if you spot a misread date, a missing town or a new gazette.</p>
  </article>
</PageLayout>
